script:
export const meta = {
  name: 'hail-b4a-swath-module',
  description: 'B4 part A, the data module: website/swath.py (listing, download, decode, crop, palette PNG, live per-tile files, words, freshness) with its tests, the gridded style and sample frames, plus the two folded docs follow-ups; two builders, two reviewers, skeptics, fixers, re-check',
  phases: [
    { title: 'Build', detail: 'swath module; follow-ups F1 and F2' },
    { title: 'Review', detail: 'data round trip and live; rules and words' },
    { title: 'Verify', detail: 'one skeptic per finding' },
    { title: 'Fix', detail: 'fixers by ownership' },
    { title: 'Recheck', detail: 'tests, live run, measurements' },
  ],
}
const WT = '/home/user/wt/wt-b4'
const BASE = '35f1233'
const BRIEF = '/home/user/wt/briefs/hail-b4-brief.md'
const B1BRIEF = '/home/user/wt/briefs/hail-build-brief.md'
const OUT = '/home/user/hail-b4/out'
const CACHE = '/home/user/hail-b4/cache/mesh-max'
const PRECHECK = '/home/user/hail-b4/precheck'
const TABLES = '/home/user/wt/mrms-tables'
const VENV = '/home/user/hail-b1/venv/bin/python'

const COMMON = `You work in the git worktree ${WT} (branch claude/hail-b4, based on the B2 work-in-progress commit ${BASE}, which holds B1, B2 and main; B2's final fixes and B3 land later by rebase, so touch no B2 file and no map or template file). This run builds only the DATA MODULE of B4 and two small docs follow-ups; the site side (build.py Collector, templates, map.js layers, the sheet's live line) is a later run. Read ${BRIEF} in full first (Product, Build, Gates B4-1 to B4-5, Ownership, the Pre-check facts, decisions D1 to D6 and the Folded follow-ups F1 and F2). Then read decisions 1 to 16 of ${B1BRIEF}, docs/DECISIONS.md D30 to D35 and docs/DESIGN.md section 8 in the worktree, website/satellite.py and website/gridded.py in full (the P2 layer pattern you reuse: STYLE, legend, the image box, the PNG pipeline, the freshness states, the HTTP helper and ledger), website/hail.py (the grid constants, pack_tenths, the class words and the two-decimal floor), and the pre-check scripts in ${PRECHECK}/ (list_and_fetch.py lists and downloads; inspect_grib.py reads the header and values; the two downloaded files are in ${CACHE}/; the MRMS GRIB2 user tables are in ${TABLES}/, rows 209/3/30 and 209/3/34). Repository rules (AGENTS.md is in your context): Python first, evidence honesty (a radar estimate is not observed hail and not damage; uncoloured is never "no hail"; stale and unavailable states say "This is not an all-clear"), feed contracts (the CONTRACTS row is the site builder's later; put its facts in your report), plain wording in sentence case, no em-dashes, no model, AI-vendor or AI-product names in repository files, tests with every behaviour change, no new pip package (pygrib behind a lazy import as gridded.py does). Before building on the live files make one listing and one download per product and check the content (header fields, grid, codes, the valid time against the file name), not just HTTP 200; record what you saw. Edit only the files you own; another builder edits other files in the same worktree now, so never run git checkout, stash, reset, add, commit or push, never reformat a file you do not own, and never delete anything outside a folder you created under ${OUT}/<your-name>/ (tests delete only folders they created). A python with numpy, pygrib 2.1.8 and pyproj is ${VENV}. Network is available. Return a plain report: what you built, the live requests you made and what their content showed, the commands you ran with results and measured numbers, and anything not done or needing a decision.`

const SWATH = `${COMMON}

You are the swath builder. You own website/swath.py (new), tests/test_swath.py (new), website/gridded.py (only the STYLE and legend additions for the new style "hail_swath", following the existing entries; nothing else changes; run tests.test_gridded after) and website/sample.py (sample frames for --sample, labelled SAMPLE as the other sample layers are). Build website/swath.py: (1) the listing per D3 (one list-type=2 request per product with the day prefix, yesterday's folder when today's is empty, the newest key), the download (gzip; one per product per build; cached by key under a cache directory), every request through the site's HTTP helper with its user agent and ledger as satellite.py does; (2) the decode with pygrib behind a lazy import: assert discipline 209, category 3, parameter 30 or 34 matching the product folder (D2), the grid 7000 by 3500 with first point 54.995 and 230.005 (B1's grid), read the valid time from dataDate and dataTime and assert it equals the file name's time (D2); longitudes are stored 0 to 360; crop to gridded.IMAGE_BOUNDS with hail.py's row and column arithmetic; (3) the values: -3 is no radar coverage (hatched), -1 and every value under 64 tenths (6.35 mm) stays uncoloured (D1; no zeros exist in these files), values at or above the floor pack with hail.pack_tenths and colour by B1's class stops in mm with their words; the PNG through gridded's palette machinery with the STYLE "hail_swath" and the legend sentence of the brief plus D1's uncoloured sentence; measure each PNG's bytes (gate B4-5: under 150 KB); (4) the live per-tile files per D4 for both windows (only tiles with a cell at or above the floor; the format of D4; measure bytes and count); (5) describe(frame, now, towns) -> the words for the List view and the no-JavaScript panel (the valid time in Montana time, the largest value in the box with its class word and exact millimetres, where: lat, lon, the nearest town and its distance in miles from towns.json, the number of cells at or above 254 tenths, the sentence that MESH is a radar estimate at the grid cell, the state line per D6 with "This is not an all-clear." on every stale or unavailable line); the sheet's templates: the live line of the Product section and D5's no-estimate line; (6) the freshness per D6 from the valid time against the build's now; the unavailable state with the plain reason on a failed listing, download, header assertion or decode; (7) WORDS_SWATH holding every sentence and template; map_data_swath(frames) -> the two layers' blocks for map.json ("hail_60" and "hail_1440": the frame URL and bounds, the legend entries with colours and words, the state and its words, the valid time, the live file URL pattern and the floor, the templates); a collect(now, cache_dir, out_dir, towns) entry point the site's Collector will call later, returning the frames, the files written and the states; (8) tests/test_swath.py with a synthetic frame (build a small GRIB2 message with pygrib or mock the message object with the keys the decode reads: say which) covering the header assertions, the valid-time check, the crop, the floor, the -3 hatch and the -1 uncoloured rule, the class colours, the live files, the words, the three freshness states, the unavailable reasons, the forbidden-term check over WORDS_SWATH (damage, confirmed, all clear, no hail, insurance, eligible, "observed hail" without "not"); run them with ${VENV} -m unittest tests.test_swath tests.test_gridded. (9) RUN the real path: ${VENV} with your module against the live bucket now (list, download, decode both products) into ${OUT}/swath/live/, and again from the cached files in ${CACHE}/ into ${OUT}/swath/cached/; report the header fields you read, the valid times, the largest values in the image box and in Montana, the PNG bytes, the live file count and bytes, the wall time of the listing, download and decode; run the live path a second time at least 30 minutes after the first (do other work between: the tests, the sample frames) and report both valid times (gate B4-2 asks for two builds apart). The sample frames in sample.py must draw both layers with SAMPLE labels through the same STYLE.`

const FOLLOWUPS = `${COMMON}

You are the follow-ups builder. You own website/storm.py, website/sources.py, tests/test_storm.py, research/globe/README.md and docs/ROADMAP.md (lines 97 and 102 only) for the two folded items of the brief's "Folded follow-ups" section, and nothing else. F1: reword the P1 code meanings to the verified ones (docs/DECISIONS.md D34 records the verification from NOAA's MRMS GRIB2 user tables, versions 12.0 to 12.2 agree, the archive files do not state their table version; in the reflectivity product -99 is missing and -999 is no coverage; read the D34 text and ${TABLES}/UserTable_MRMS_v12.2.csv row for MergedReflectivityQC to quote the right words): storm.py lines 11 to 14 (docstring), the NO_DATA comment at line 60, the sentence built at line 243, and sources.py CONTRACTS["storm_layers"] "missing" at line 142; keep "never a value, never 0"; keep the clear-night product question open (ROADMAP line 102); update tests/test_storm.py lines 235 and 565 (and any other assertion on the old sentence) to the new wording; no behaviour change; run ${VENV} -m unittest tests.test_storm tests.test_site and quote the tail. F2: in research/globe/README.md rename the proposal labels D33 to D40 to GP-1 to GP-8 (globe proposal) throughout the file (grep every occurrence, including the table at lines 543 to 545 and sections 2, 4, 6, 8 and the freshness tables), keep their content, add one sentence under that table: "GP numbers are proposals from this plan; D numbers are decisions recorded in docs/DECISIONS.md."; in docs/ROADMAP.md line 97 mark the -99/-999 check done (D34) and at line 102 keep the clear-night ruling open; nothing else in the globe plan changes. Em-dashes 0 in your lines (the README may already contain some: do not add any; leave existing text alone except the labels); no model or AI-vendor names. Report the diff stat and a grep showing no D33 to D40 proposal label remains in the globe README.`

const FINDINGS = { type: 'object', properties: { findings: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'object', properties: { id: { type: 'string' }, file: { type: 'string' }, line: { type: 'integer' }, claim: { type: 'string' }, evidence: { type: 'string' }, fix: { type: 'string' }, severity: { type: 'string', enum: ['blocking', 'should', 'minor'] }, confidence: { type: 'number' } }, required: ['id', 'file', 'line', 'claim', 'evidence', 'fix', 'severity', 'confidence'] } }, measurements: { type: 'object', additionalProperties: true }, gates: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'object', properties: { gate: { type: 'string' }, status: { type: 'string', enum: ['pass', 'fail', 'not_checked'] }, evidence: { type: 'string' } }, required: ['gate', 'status', 'evidence'] } }, summary: { type: 'string' } }, required: ['findings', 'measurements', 'gates', 'summary'] }
const VERDICT = { type: 'object', properties: { id: { type: 'string' }, refuted: { type: 'boolean' }, reason: { type: 'string' }, fix_ok: { type: 'boolean' }, adjusted_fix: { type: 'string' } }, required: ['id', 'refuted', 'reason', 'fix_ok', 'adjusted_fix'] }

const REVIEW_COMMON = `Read-only reviewer in ${WT} (branch claude/hail-b4). Read ${BRIEF} first (the contract, gates B4-1 to B4-5, the pre-check facts, decisions D1 to D6, the folded follow-ups F1 and F2), then decisions 1 to 16 of ${B1BRIEF}. The downloaded files are in ${CACHE}/ and the pre-check scripts in ${PRECHECK}/ (an independent reader you may reuse); the MRMS tables are in ${TABLES}/. Never edit, stage, commit or push; scratch only under ${OUT}/<your-name>/ which you create; never delete anything outside it. Report concrete defects only, each with file, line, evidence and an exact minimal fix; rank blocking first; fill the gates you checked; put every measured number in measurements.`
const REVIEWERS = [
  { key: 'data', prompt: `${REVIEW_COMMON} Lens: data correctness. Read website/swath.py, tests/test_swath.py and the gridded.py and sample.py changes in full. Then: (1) gate B4-1: with your own pygrib read of the files under ${OUT}/swath/live/ (and ${CACHE}/), decode independently, crop to gridded.IMAGE_BOUNDS yourself, and at 1,000 sampled pixels of each PNG compare the palette class with the class of your decoded value (0 mismatches expected; state the sampling); check every cell at or above 64 tenths in the box appears in the live per-tile files with the right tenths and tile name, and nothing below the floor does; (2) gate B4-2: list and download the newest file of each product now, decode, confirm the header (209/3/30 and 209/3/34, grid 7000 by 3500, first point 54.995 and 230.005), the valid time equals the file name's time, the codes present (-1, -3, no zeros) and the units against ${TABLES}/UserTable_MRMS_v12.2.csv; compare with the builder's two recorded runs; (3) the floor and class stops equal B1's (hail.py) in tenths and mm; the two-decimal floor and exact millimetres in the words; (4) the freshness thresholds in code equal D6; the state on a synthetic now 25, 65 and 125 minutes after the valid time for each layer; (5) the unavailable paths: parameter 28, a file name time differing from the header, a failed listing (fake), a failed download; each gives state unavailable with a plain reason and no frame; (6) the sample frames: SAMPLE labelled, through the same STYLE; (7) determinism: two decodes of the same file give byte-identical PNGs and live files.` },
  { key: 'rules', prompt: `${REVIEW_COMMON} Lens: repository rules and words over the whole diff (git -C ${WT} diff ${BASE} --stat, the full diff, untracked files). Check: evidence honesty (every colour carries its word; "not observed hail", "not damage" and "This is not an all-clear" where the brief puts them; uncoloured never reads as "no hail"; the forbidden-term check in the tests covers WORDS_SWATH); the words for the site builder's CONTRACTS row are in the report (cadences, units, codes per D1, the floor, the valid time rule); tests exist for every behaviour and ${VENV} -m unittest discover -s tests passes (quote the tail) and python desktop/launcher.py --selftest payload passes; gridded.py changed only in STYLE and legend entries (diff it) and tests.test_gridded passes; em-dashes in added lines 0; the words Claude, Anthropic, Codex, ChatGPT, OpenAI, Copilot, Gemini, Haiku, Opus, Sonnet in added lines only inside branch names; plain wording and sentence case; no new pip package (pygrib behind a lazy import); decision 7 audit: every rmtree, unlink or os.remove under tests/ acts on a folder the test created; F1: the new wording in storm.py and sources.py matches D34's text and the table row (quote all three), tests updated, no behaviour change (diff the logic); F2: grep research/globe/README.md for D33 to D40 proposal labels (none should remain except where the text names a real decision in docs/DECISIONS.md: say which), the added sentence present, ROADMAP lines 97 and 102 as the brief says, no other line of the globe plan changed (diff --stat).` },
]
const OWNERSHIP = { swath: ['website/swath.py', 'tests/test_swath.py', 'website/gridded.py', 'website/sample.py', 'tests/test_gridded.py'], followups: ['website/storm.py', 'website/sources.py', 'tests/test_storm.py', 'research/globe/README.md', 'docs/ROADMAP.md'] }
function ownerOf(file) { for (const [k, ps] of Object.entries(OWNERSHIP)) if (ps.some((p) => file.startsWith(p) || file.includes(p))) return k; return 'swath' }

phase('Build')
const [swathR, followR] = await parallel([
  () => agent(SWATH, { label: 'build:swath', phase: 'Build', model: 'haiku' }),
  () => agent(FOLLOWUPS, { label: 'build:followups', phase: 'Build', model: 'haiku' }),
])
log(`builders done: swath ${swathR ? 'ok' : 'null'}, followups ${followR ? 'ok' : 'null'}`)

phase('Review')
const reviews = await pipeline(
  REVIEWERS,
  (r) => agent(`${r.prompt}\n\nBuilder reports for context (claims to check, not facts):\nSWATH: ${(swathR || 'none').slice(0, 7000)}\nFOLLOWUPS: ${(followR || 'none').slice(0, 4000)}`, { label: `review:${r.key}`, phase: 'Review', schema: FINDINGS, model: 'haiku' }),
  async (result, r) => {
    if (!result) return { key: r.key, findings: [], measurements: {}, gates: [], summary: 'reviewer returned nothing' }
    const fs = result.findings.map((f, i) => ({ ...f, id: `${r.key}-${i + 1}` }))
    log(`${r.key}: ${fs.length} findings`)
    const verdicts = await parallel(fs.map((f) => () =>
      agent(`You are a skeptic checking one review finding in ${WT} (read-only; never edit, stage, commit or push; scratch only under ${OUT}/skeptic-${f.id}/ which you create). The finding:\n${JSON.stringify(f, null, 1)}\n\nOpen the file at the line, reproduce the observation yourself, and read ${BRIEF} (decisions D1 to D6, follow-ups F1 and F2). The finding stands only if the defect is real, matters to gates B4-1 to B4-5, the follow-ups or the repository rules (AGENTS.md), and the proposed fix is correct and minimal and introduces nothing unsupported (no new number, no em-dash, no model or AI-product name, no new pip package, no contradiction of the brief's decisions). If the defect is real but the fix is wrong or too broad, set fix_ok false and give an adjusted_fix. Default to refuted=true when uncertain.`,
        { label: `verify:${f.id}`, phase: 'Verify', schema: VERDICT, model: 'haiku' }).then((v) => (v ? { ...f, verdict: v } : null))))
    return { key: r.key, findings: verdicts.filter(Boolean), measurements: result.measurements, gates: result.gates, summary: result.summary }
  },
)
const all = reviews.flatMap((r) => r.findings)
const confirmed = all.filter((f) => !f.verdict.refuted).map((f) => ({ id: f.id, file: f.file, line: f.line, claim: f.claim, evidence: f.evidence, severity: f.severity, fix: f.verdict.fix_ok ? f.fix : f.verdict.adjusted_fix, owner: ownerOf(f.file) })).filter((f) => f.fix && f.fix.length > 0)
const refuted = all.filter((f) => f.verdict.refuted).map((f) => ({ id: f.id, file: f.file, claim: f.claim, reason: f.verdict.reason }))
log(`confirmed ${confirmed.length} (blocking ${confirmed.filter((f) => f.severity === 'blocking').length}), refuted ${refuted.length}`)

phase('Fix')
const groups = ['swath', 'followups'].map((k) => ({ k, items: confirmed.filter((f) => f.owner === k) })).filter((g) => g.items.length)
const fixReports = await parallel(groups.map((g) => () => agent(`${COMMON}\n\nYou are the fixer for the ${g.k} files; you own exactly: ${OWNERSHIP[g.k].join(', ')}. Apply these confirmed findings with exact, minimal edits that keep every public name and the brief's contract (decisions D1 to D6 decide any choice; a finding that contradicts one is skipped with the decision cited). Skip and report any finding naming a file you do not own. After editing run ${VENV} -m unittest tests.test_swath tests.test_gridded tests.test_storm tests.test_site. Report applied ids, skipped ids with reasons, and the test tail.\n\nFINDINGS:\n${JSON.stringify(g.items, null, 1)}`, { label: `fix:${g.k}`, phase: 'Fix', model: 'haiku' })))

phase('Recheck')
const recheck = await agent(`${REVIEW_COMMON} You are the final checker of this data-module run. Run and quote: ${VENV} -m unittest discover -s tests (tail); python desktop/launcher.py --selftest payload; the module's live path now into ${OUT}/recheck/live (list, download, decode both products; the header fields and valid times; PNG bytes; live file count and bytes); the round trip at 1,000 pixels per layer with your own decode (0 mismatches expected); the sample frames through the STYLE; git -C ${WT} status --short and diff --stat ${BASE}; em-dash and banned-name counts over added lines; the decision 7 audit; the F1 wording (quote the four places) and the F2 grep. Write ${OUT}/b4a-recheck.json with gates B4-1 to B4-5 (status, evidence), the measurements, F1 and F2 status, and the applied and refuted findings (ids, one line each). Return that JSON as text.`, { label: 'recheck', phase: 'Recheck', model: 'haiku' })

return { swathR: (swathR || '').slice(0, 8000), followR: (followR || '').slice(0, 4000), reviews: reviews.map((r) => ({ key: r.key, summary: r.summary, gates: r.gates, measurements: r.measurements })), confirmed, refuted, fixReports, recheck }
